  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "A function of the form $(ax+b)^{n}$ is a power of a **linear bracket**. To find its primitive, add $1$ to the power, then divide by both the new power and the coefficient $a$.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\displaystyle\\int(ax+b)^{n}\\,dx=\\dfrac{(ax+b)^{n+1}}{a(n+1)}+C$ for every real $n\\ne-1$.",
      "The extra division by $a$ undoes the factor of $a$ that the chain rule produces.",
      "Leave the bracket alone; never expand $(ax+b)^{n}$ before integrating.",
      "When $a$ is negative the primitive is negative, since $a(n+1)$ is then negative.",
      "A coefficient in front multiplies the whole primitive: $\\displaystyle\\int k(ax+b)^{n}\\,dx=\\dfrac{k(ax+b)^{n+1}}{a(n+1)}+C$.",
      "Rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{(ax+b)^{m}}$ as $(ax+b)^{-m}$ before integrating.",
      "Rewrite $\\sqrt{ax+b}$ as $(ax+b)^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ before integrating.",
      "The rule fails at $n=-1$, since $a(n+1)$ would be zero.",
      "Check the answer by differentiating with the chain rule, which should return $(ax+b)^{n}$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rewrite roots and bracket denominators as $(ax+b)^{n}$ with a single index",
      "Add $1$ to the power to get $(ax+b)^{n+1}$",
      "Divide by the new power $n+1$ and by the coefficient $a$",
      "Add the constant of integration $+C$"
    ]
  },
